% ============================================================================
% farm-animal-maintenance-level — for the farm animal whose SAME cited
% CFSPH span also names its husbandry difficulty, that maintenance level
% (adj-facts-stdlib, AGRICULTURE).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `farm-animals.adj` (`farm_animal_product
% (animal, product)`) and `farm-animal-secondary-product.adj`
% (`farm_animal_secondary_product(animal, product)`). Sheep's own span is
% the SAME sentence `farm-animals.adj` already carries as its provenance
% envelope's `source` field -- but only the trailing clause ("...they can
% produce wool, meat, and milk") was ever decoded into rows; the LEADING
% clause of that same sentence names a husbandry-difficulty fact about the
% animal itself, never decoded anywhere:
%
%   sheep → low_maintenance
%     "Sheep are low maintenance and versatile – depending on the breed,
%      they can produce wool, meat, and milk"
%
% "Maintenance level" (how much care the ANIMAL needs) is categorically
% distinct from "product" (what the animal GIVES) -- the two existing
% tables both decode only the product half of this sentence; the
% husbandry-difficulty half has never been shipped. (The sentence's other
% unused word, "versatile," is deliberately NOT decoded into a second row:
% it is trivially re-derivable from the fact that sheep already carry
% three rows across the two existing product tables -- wool, meat, milk --
% so asserting it separately would restate rather than add a fact.)
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? farm_animal_maintenance_level(sheep, $Level)   % low_maintenance
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `farm_animal_maintenance_level(animal, level)` carrying the citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the engine returns
% the maintenance level WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on every other animal `farm-animals.adj` carries,
% since none of the other four cited spans (chicken, duck, sheep's OWN
% product clause is separate from this fact, rabbit, goat) states a
% husbandry-difficulty descriptor. The query also runs BACKWARD -- bind a
% level and recall which animal it names:
%
%     ? farm_animal_maintenance_level($Animal, low_maintenance)   % sheep
%
% Only ONE of `farm-animals.adj`'s five animals has a genuinely distinct
% maintenance-level fact in the ALREADY-cited spans; this table is
% deliberately narrow (a single row, the same shape as several other
% already-shipped single-row siblings in this stdlib, e.g.
% `agriculture/farm-animal-product-processing.adj`,
% `anatomy/valve-alternate-name.adj`) rather than inventing a level the
% source does not state.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME sheep sentence already
% quoted inside `farm-animals.adj`'s own `source` field -- no new
% WebFetch, only a different schema decoding the husbandry-difficulty half
% of that already-verified quote. `trust authoritative` -- the same tier
% `farm-animals.adj` already carries (Iowa State University CFSPH, a
% primary university educational source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table farm_animal_maintenance_level {
    columns animal, level

    row (sheep, low_maintenance)   % CFSPH: "Sheep are low maintenance and versatile..."

    source "Sheep are low maintenance and versatile – depending on the breed, they can produce wool, meat, and milk"
    locator "https://www.cfsph.iastate.edu/thelivestockproject/animals-for-beginning-small-farmers/"
    trust authoritative
}
